DSP.mod(13,{"m13-metrics":{"id":"m13-metrics","slug":"metric-vocabulary","m":13,"title":"Metric vocabulary","min":25,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":[],"tags":["product-metrics","business"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"The metric vocabulary of product, e-commerce, marketing, fintech and marketplace companies, with precise definitions, worked calculations and metric hygiene.","keyTerms":["DAU MAU stickiness","retention","churn","GMV","AOV","conversion","CAC","LTV","NPS","default rate","Goodhart's law"],"interviewQs":["Define DAU/MAU stickiness and compute it.","What is LTV and how is it related to CAC?","What could go wrong with optimising a single metric?"],"mistakes":["Vague metric definitions (no denominator, no time window)","Confusing GMV with revenue","Ignoring Goodhart's law"],"practice":"Pick an app you use and define five metrics (one per area) with numerator, denominator, window and unit.","related":["m13-metric-drop","m13-feature-success","m6-lifecycle"],"points":"Area - Metrics\nGrowth/engagement - DAU, WAU, MAU, stickiness (DAU/MAU), sessions per user, time spent, activation rate\nRetention - D1/D7/D30 retention, cohort retention, churn rate, resurrection rate\nE-commerce/quick-commerce - GMV, orders, AOV, conversion rate, cart abandonment, repeat rate, fill rate, delivery time (P50/P90), cancellation rate, NPS, take rate\nMarketing - CAC, ROAS, CTR, CPC, CPA, conversion funnel, attribution\nRevenue/SaaS - ARPU, LTV, LTV:CAC, MRR/ARR, net revenue retention\nFintech/lending - Approval rate, default rate, NPA, delinquency buckets (DPD), collection efficiency, TPV, fraud rate, chargebacks\nMarketplace - Supply-demand balance, liquidity, utilization, match rate\nOps - SLA adherence, cost per order, first-contact resolution\n\nMetric hygiene: define numerator/denominator precisely, time window, unit (user/order), inclusion rules, and watch for gaming and Goodhart's law."},"intuitive":"<p>Metrics are the company&#39;s dashboard lights. &quot;Are people using it?&quot; (DAU, MAU), &quot;do they come back?&quot; (retention), &quot;are they buying?&quot; (conversion, AOV), &quot;is it worth what we pay to get them?&quot; (CAC vs LTV), &quot;is the service good?&quot; (delivery time, NPS). The trick is to define each number exactly: what is counted, out of what, over what time, for which users. And remember: when a number becomes a target, people (and algorithms) start gaming it.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Case rounds start by asking for the right metrics; vague definitions are an immediate red flag.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Always state numerator, denominator, window and unit, and pair every target metric with a guardrail.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;A metric turns behaviour into a number. I separate three kinds. The north star is the single metric that captures delivered value, like weekly active buyers. Input or driver metrics are the levers that move it, such as search-to-click rate. Guardrails are what must not break, like latency or refund rate. Common ones: DAU and MAU, retention, conversion, ARPU, CAC and LTV, churn, NPS. Good metrics are measurable, sensitive, hard to game and tied to a decision. Ratios need both numerator and denominator thought through. The catch: a metric optimised alone gets gamed, such as clicks rising while satisfaction falls, so I always pair it with a guardrail.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Build a metric tree for a food-delivery app","html":"<ol>\n<li><strong>North star: weekly completed orders.</strong> It reflects value for customers (they got food), restaurants (they sold) and the company (it earned).</li>\n<li><strong>Break it into a product of drivers:</strong> weekly orders = weekly active users × orders per active user.</li>\n<li><strong>Go one level deeper:</strong> weekly active users = new users + returning users; orders per active user depends on app conversion (sessions that end in an order) and on how often people come back.</li>\n<li><strong>Put numbers on it:</strong> 200,000 active users × 1.5 orders each = 300,000 orders. A 10% rise in conversion with no change in users gives about 330,000.</li>\n<li><strong>Add guardrails</strong> that must not get worse while the drivers improve: delivery time, cancellation rate, restaurant ratings and contribution margin per order.</li>\n<li><strong>Give each team a driver.</strong> Growth owns new users; the search team owns conversion; operations owns delivery time. Everyone can see how their number feeds the north star.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Metric</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>North star</td><td>Weekly completed orders</td><td>the value delivered</td></tr><tr><td>Drivers</td><td>Active users, orders per user, conversion</td><td>levers teams can move</td></tr><tr><td>Guardrails</td><td>Delivery time, cancellations, margin</td><td>stop harmful growth</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A metric tree turns one goal into levers people can act on, and the guardrails stop a team from moving its lever in a way that damages the whole.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-vocabulary-with-definitions-and-examples","text":"The vocabulary, with definitions and examples","level":3},{"id":"h-worked-calculations","text":"Worked calculations","level":3},{"id":"h-metric-hygiene","text":"Metric hygiene","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Metrics</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Growth/engagement</td><td>DAU, WAU, MAU, stickiness (DAU/MAU), sessions per user, time spent, activation rate</td></tr><tr><td>Retention</td><td>D1/D7/D30 retention, cohort retention, churn rate, resurrection rate</td></tr><tr><td>E-commerce/quick-commerce</td><td>GMV, orders, AOV, conversion rate, cart abandonment, repeat rate, fill rate, delivery time (P50/P90), cancellation rate, NPS, take rate</td></tr><tr><td>Marketing</td><td>CAC, ROAS, CTR, CPC, CPA, conversion funnel, attribution</td></tr><tr><td>Revenue/SaaS</td><td>ARPU, LTV, LTV:CAC, MRR/ARR, net revenue retention</td></tr><tr><td>Fintech/lending</td><td>Approval rate, default rate, NPA, delinquency buckets (DPD), collection efficiency, TPV, fraud rate, chargebacks</td></tr><tr><td>Marketplace</td><td>Supply-demand balance, liquidity, utilization, match rate</td></tr><tr><td>Ops</td><td>SLA adherence, cost per order, first-contact resolution</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Metric hygiene:</strong> define numerator/denominator precisely, time window, unit (user/order), inclusion rules, and watch for gaming and Goodhart&#39;s law.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-vocabulary-with-definitions-and-examples\">The vocabulary, with definitions and examples</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Metric</th><th>Definition / example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Growth / engagement</strong></td><td>DAU, WAU, MAU</td><td>unique active users per day / week / month (define &quot;active&quot;: opened the app? placed an order?)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td><strong>Stickiness</strong></td><td>DAU / MAU: if DAU = 2M and MAU = 8M, stickiness = 25% (users active on about 7.5 days a month)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Activation rate</td><td>share of new users completing a key first action (first order within 7 days)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Sessions per user, time spent</td><td>engagement depth</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Retention</strong></td><td>D1 / D7 / D30 retention</td><td>share of a cohort active exactly on day 1 / 7 / 30 after joining</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Churn rate</td><td>share of customers lost in a period (1 − retention for the period)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Resurrection rate</td><td>previously churned users who return</td></tr><tr><td><strong>E-commerce / quick commerce</strong></td><td><strong>GMV</strong></td><td>gross merchandise value: total value of goods sold <em>before</em> returns, discounts and costs; <strong>not</strong> revenue</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Orders, <strong>AOV</strong></td><td>AOV = GMV / orders</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Conversion rate</td><td>orders / sessions (or checkouts / cart sessions: state the funnel step)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Cart abandonment</td><td>1 − purchases / carts</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Repeat rate</td><td>share of customers with 2+ orders in a window</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Fill rate</td><td>share of ordered items actually fulfilled</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Delivery time P50 / P90</td><td>median and 90th percentile promised-vs-actual; use percentiles, not averages</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Cancellation rate, NPS</td><td>quality signals</td></tr><tr><td></td><td><strong>Take rate</strong></td><td>marketplace revenue / GMV (commission share)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Marketing</strong></td><td>CAC</td><td>acquisition spend / new customers acquired</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>ROAS</td><td>revenue attributed to ads / ad spend</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>CTR, CPC, CPA</td><td>click-through rate; cost per click; cost per acquisition</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Revenue / SaaS</strong></td><td>ARPU, <strong>LTV</strong>, <strong>LTV:CAC</strong></td><td>average revenue per user; lifetime value; a healthy ratio is often cited as 3:1 or better</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>MRR / ARR, net revenue retention</td><td>recurring revenue and expansion minus churn</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fintech / lending</strong></td><td>Approval rate, default rate, NPA</td><td>approval share; share defaulting; non-performing assets</td></tr><tr><td></td><td><strong>DPD buckets</strong>, collection efficiency</td><td>days past due (30/60/90+), share of due amounts collected</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>TPV, fraud rate, chargebacks</td><td>total payment volume; fraud losses</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Marketplace</strong></td><td>supply-demand balance, liquidity, utilisation, match rate</td><td>health of both sides</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Operations</strong></td><td>SLA adherence, cost per order, first-contact resolution</td><td>efficiency</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-worked-calculations\">Worked calculations</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>LTV (simple):</strong> average margin per order × orders per year × expected lifetime in years. If a customer yields ₹120 margin per order, orders 10 times a year, and lasts 3 years, LTV ≈ ₹3,600; with a CAC of ₹900 the LTV:CAC is 4:1.</li>\n<li><strong>Retention to LTV:</strong> with monthly retention of 90%, expected lifetime is 1 / (1 − 0.90) = 10 months; at 80% it is only 5 months. A small retention change has a large effect on LTV.</li>\n<li><strong>GMV vs revenue:</strong> ₹100 crore GMV with a 15% take rate yields ₹15 crore of net revenue for the platform.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-metric-hygiene\">Metric hygiene</h3>\n<p>For every metric write the <strong>numerator, denominator, time window, unit</strong> (user, order, session), <strong>inclusion rules</strong> (test accounts? cancelled orders?), and <strong>owner</strong>. Watch for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Goodhart&#39;s law:</strong> when a measure becomes a target it stops being a good measure (optimising delivery time by cancelling hard orders; optimising clicks with clickbait). Pair with a <strong>guardrail</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Averages hiding tails:</strong> report percentiles for latency and delivery times.</li>\n<li><strong>Simpson-type mix shifts:</strong> totals move because the mix of users changed.</li>\n<li><strong>Survivorship and cohort mixing:</strong> compare like-aged cohorts.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m13-metrics~1","q":"DAU is 1.5M and MAU is 6M. The CEO says &quot;stickiness is too low&quot;. Is 25% low?","a":"<p>Stickiness = DAU/MAU = 1.5 / 6 = 25%, meaning the average monthly user is active about 7.5 days a month. Whether that is low depends on the <strong>natural usage frequency</strong>: a messaging or news app should target 50% or more; a fashion or grocery-restock app can be healthy at 15 to 30%. Compare with your own trend and peers, look at cohorts and the distribution of days active (power users vs occasionals), and define what &quot;active&quot; means before judging.</p>\n","s":"It depends on the product: a daily-use app should be much higher; a weekly-use service can be healthy at 25%."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m13-metrics~ca0_1","q":"A video app optimises &#39;watch time&#39;. What guardrail would you add and why?","a":"<p>User satisfaction or return rate, or complaint/skip rates, because watch time can rise from addictive or low-quality content that hurts retention.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m13-metrics~ca0_2","q":"DAU/MAU of 0.2 means what?","a":"<p>On an average day, 20% of monthly users are active (stickiness): they use the product about 6 days a month.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m13-metrics~ca0_3","q":"Why is a ratio metric like conversion rate tricky in an experiment?","a":"<p>Both numerator and denominator can change; the unit of analysis must match the unit of randomisation, and variance needs the delta method or per-user metrics.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m13-metrics~ta1_1","q":"<p>An app has 1,200,000 monthly active users and 300,000 average daily active users. Compute DAU/MAU and explain what it means in plain words.</p>\n","a":"<p>DAU/MAU = 300,000 / 1,200,000 = 0.25. On an average day a quarter of the month&#39;s users show up; equivalently, a typical monthly user is active on about 0.25 × 30 ≈ 7.5 days a month. It measures stickiness: a messaging app might be above 0.5, a travel-booking app far lower.</p>\n"}]},"m13-metric-drop":{"id":"m13-metric-drop","slug":"framework-diagnosing-a-metric-drop","m":13,"title":"Framework: diagnosing a metric drop","min":30,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":["m13-metrics"],"tags":["product-metrics","case-study","framework"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"A structured framework for diagnosing a metric drop, with a worked decomposition and segmentation example, the order of checks, and how to communicate findings.","keyTerms":["metric decomposition","segmentation","funnel analysis","internal vs external causes","hypothesis prioritisation","data validation"],"interviewQs":["Orders dropped 10% week over week. How do you investigate?","How do you decide whether a drop is real?","What would you check first?"],"mistakes":["Jumping to causes without verifying the data","Listing ideas without structure or prioritisation","Forgetting external factors (festivals, weather, matches)"],"practice":"Answer the \"orders dropped 10%\" question aloud in three minutes using the seven steps, then compare with the worked example.","related":["m13-metrics","m2-level-4b","m13-feature-success"],"points":"Question: \"Orders dropped 10% week over week. How do you investigate?\"\n\n1. Clarify and verify: Is it real? Check the data pipeline, logging, definitions, dashboards lagging. Size and timing (sudden vs gradual).\n2. Decompose the metric: Orders = Users x Sessions/User x Conversion x Orders/Converter. Find which component moved.\n3. Segment: platform (Android/iOS/web), city/region, new vs returning users, category, acquisition channel, time of day, app version.\n4. Internal causes: releases/bugs, pricing or fee changes, promotions ended, supply issues (stockouts, fewer delivery partners), ranking/recommendation changes, experiments.\n5. External causes: seasonality, festivals, holidays, weather, competitor offers, cricket matches/events, policy/regulatory changes, payment gateway outages.\n6. Funnel analysis: where did drop-off increase (search -> product -> cart -> payment -> success)?\n7. Form hypotheses, test them with data, prioritize by impact, recommend actions and monitoring."},"intuitive":"<p>When a key number suddenly drops, act like a detective, not a fortune teller. First make sure the drop is real and not a broken chart. Then split the number into its parts to find where it fell (fewer visitors? fewer of them buying?). Then slice by groups (which phones, cities, new vs old customers) to find where it is concentrated. Only then list the possible causes (something we changed, something outside our control), check the evidence for each, and recommend what to do.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> This is the single most common product-analytics case question.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Verify, decompose, segment, hypothesise from the evidence, prioritise, act, monitor.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;When a metric drops, I do not guess; I follow a framework. First, verify: is the data real, or a logging, pipeline or definition bug? Second, scope it: when did it start, is it gradual or a step, and is it everywhere? Third, decompose the metric into parts, like orders equal visitors times conversion, and see which part moved. Fourth, segment by platform, region, new versus returning, channel and product. Fifth, check internal causes such as releases, experiments, price changes and outages, and external ones such as seasonality, holidays, competitors. Then form hypotheses, test the likeliest and recommend an action. The catch: I start with the cheapest checks, data issues and recent releases.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Decompose a 10% drop in orders","html":"<p>Orders fell from 10,000 last week to 9,000 this week.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Is it real?</strong> The dashboard query is unchanged, the pipeline ran, and the payment logs show the same drop. It is real.</li>\n<li><strong>Write orders as a product:</strong> orders = sessions × conversion.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Last week</th><th>This week</th><th>Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sessions</td><td>50,000</td><td>49,500</td><td>−1%</td></tr><tr><td>Conversion</td><td>20.0%</td><td>18.2%</td><td>−9%</td></tr><tr><td>Orders</td><td>10,000</td><td>9,000</td><td>−10%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li><strong>Read it.</strong> Traffic barely moved; conversion fell. People are arriving but not completing orders. The problem is inside the app, not in marketing.</li>\n<li><strong>Segment conversion by platform:</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Last week</th><th>This week</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>iOS (20,000 sessions)</td><td>20.0%</td><td>20.0%</td></tr><tr><td>Android (about 30,000 sessions)</td><td>20.0%</td><td>16.9%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li><strong>Narrow it further.</strong> Only Android fell. Check the release calendar: Android version 4.2 shipped on Monday. Look at the Android funnel: is the drop at payment?</li>\n<li><strong>Recommend:</strong> roll back or hotfix 4.2&#39;s payment screen, add an alert on conversion by platform, and estimate the lost orders (about 1,000 for the week) for the incident report.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Verify, decompose, segment, find the cause, recommend. Writing the metric as a product points straight at the component that moved.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-seven-steps","text":"The seven steps","level":3},{"id":"h-a-worked-example","text":"A worked example","level":3},{"id":"h-how-to-communicate","text":"How to communicate","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Question: &quot;Orders dropped 10% week over week. How do you investigate?&quot;</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ol>\n<li><strong>Clarify and verify:</strong> Is it real? Check the data pipeline, logging, definitions, dashboards lagging. Size and timing (sudden vs gradual).</li>\n<li><strong>Decompose the metric:</strong> Orders = Users x Sessions/User x Conversion x Orders/Converter. Find which component moved.</li>\n<li><strong>Segment:</strong> platform (Android/iOS/web), city/region, new vs returning users, category, acquisition channel, time of day, app version.</li>\n<li><strong>Internal causes:</strong> releases/bugs, pricing or fee changes, promotions ended, supply issues (stockouts, fewer delivery partners), ranking/recommendation changes, experiments.</li>\n<li><strong>External causes:</strong> seasonality, festivals, holidays, weather, competitor offers, cricket matches/events, policy/regulatory changes, payment gateway outages.</li>\n<li><strong>Funnel analysis:</strong> where did drop-off increase (search -&gt; product -&gt; cart -&gt; payment -&gt; success)?</li>\n<li><strong>Form hypotheses, test them with data, prioritize by impact, recommend actions and monitoring.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-seven-steps\">The seven steps</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Clarify and verify.</strong> Is it real? Check the data pipeline, logging changes, dashboard definitions and lag. Size and timing (sudden vs gradual; one day or the whole week?).</li>\n<li><strong>Decompose the metric.</strong> <code>Orders = Users × Sessions per user × Conversion × Orders per converter</code> (or <code>Orders = Active customers × Order frequency</code>). Find which factor moved.</li>\n<li><strong>Segment.</strong> Platform (Android, iOS, web), city or region, new vs returning users, category, acquisition channel, time of day, app version.</li>\n<li><strong>Internal causes.</strong> Releases and bugs, pricing or fee changes, promotions that ended, supply problems (stock-outs, fewer delivery partners), ranking or recommendation changes, running experiments.</li>\n<li><strong>External causes.</strong> Seasonality, festivals and holidays, weather, competitor offers, cricket matches and events, policy or regulation, payment gateway outages.</li>\n<li><strong>Funnel analysis.</strong> Where did drop-off increase: search, product page, cart, payment, success?</li>\n<li><strong>Form hypotheses, test with data, prioritise by impact, recommend actions and monitoring.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"h-a-worked-example\">A worked example</h3>\n<p>Orders fell 10% week over week (from 500,000 to 450,000). Decompose with the data:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Last week</th><th>This week</th><th>Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sessions</td><td>4.00 M</td><td>3.98 M</td><td>−0.5%</td></tr><tr><td>Session to order conversion</td><td>12.5%</td><td>11.3%</td><td><strong>−9.6%</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Orders</td><td>500,000</td><td>450,000</td><td>−10.0%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Traffic is steady, so the problem is <strong>conversion</strong>, not acquisition. Segment conversion:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Segment</th><th>Conversion last week</th><th>This week</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Android, app version 4.2</td><td>12.6%</td><td><strong>9.1%</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Android, other versions</td><td>12.5%</td><td>12.4%</td></tr><tr><td>iOS</td><td>12.4%</td><td>12.3%</td></tr><tr><td>Web</td><td>12.3%</td><td>12.3%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The drop is concentrated in <strong>Android version 4.2</strong>, a release that shipped on Monday. The funnel shows payment success on that version fell from 94% to 81%. Hypothesis: a bug in the payment SDK integration. Verify by checking error logs for that version and payment gateway responses, and by comparing with users on earlier versions in the same cities. <strong>Action:</strong> hotfix or roll back, compensate affected users, add a release gate on payment success rate.</p>\n<p>If instead every segment had dropped evenly, the next suspects would be external (festival, weather, a competitor campaign) or a platform-wide change (pricing, ranking).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-how-to-communicate\">How to communicate</h3>\n<p>Lead with the answer (&quot;the drop is a payment bug on Android 4.2, affecting about 40,000 orders&quot;), then the evidence (decomposition, segment chart), the confidence and what you did not check, then the recommendation and follow-up monitoring.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m13-metric-drop~1","q":"Weekly active users of a fitness app dropped 15% in the first week of January compared with the last week of December. What do you think?","a":"<p>Verify the data and <strong>compare with the same week in previous years</strong>: late December can show unusual usage (holiday campaigns, New Year&#39;s resolutions starting later) and January often has its own pattern, so a week-over-week comparison across a holiday boundary is misleading. Segment by new vs returning users and by acquisition channel, check campaigns that ended, app releases and notification changes. If it is seasonal, the right comparison is year over year or against an expected baseline; if not, follow the decomposition and segmentation steps.</p>\n","s":"First check whether it is seasonal (holiday spikes), then segment and compare with prior years before calling it a problem."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m13-metric-drop~ca0_1","q":"Orders dropped 10% yesterday. What do you check first?","a":"<p>Whether the data is correct (pipeline, tracking, definitions), then whether it is a step change at a specific time, which suggests a release or outage.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m13-metric-drop~ca0_2","q":"How do decomposition and segmentation differ?","a":"<p>Decomposition splits the metric into components (traffic × conversion × basket); segmentation splits by user or context (platform, country) to find where it moved.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m13-metric-drop~ca0_3","q":"The drop is only on Android after the 4.2 release. Next step?","a":"<p>Check release notes and crash or funnel data for Android 4.2, reproduce the bug, and roll back or hotfix.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m13-metric-drop~ta1_1","q":"<p>Revenue fell 8% week on week. Visitors are flat, conversion is flat, and orders are flat. Where is the problem, and what would you check next?</p>\n","a":"<p>Revenue = visitors × conversion × average order value. With the first two flat (and orders flat), the drop must be in average order value. Check: a price or discount change, a promotion pushing cheaper items, a shift in product or category mix, a change in delivery fees counted in revenue, or a change in how revenue is calculated (taxes or refunds). Then segment order value by category and city.</p>\n"}]},"m13-feature-success":{"id":"m13-feature-success","slug":"framework-measuring-the-success-of-a-feature","m":13,"title":"Framework: measuring the success of a feature","min":35,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":["m13-metrics","m6-lifecycle"],"tags":["product-metrics","experimentation","case-study"],"qa":8,"ai":{"scope":"A framework for measuring the success of a product feature, including metric hierarchy, experiment design, quasi-experimental alternatives, and model answers to four practice prompts.","keyTerms":["success metrics","north star","leading and lagging indicators","guardrails","cannibalisation","quasi-experiments","difference-in-differences"],"interviewQs":["How would you measure the success of a new \"reorder\" button?","Should Zomato increase delivery fees in rainy weather?","How do you evaluate a loyalty programme when you cannot run an A/B test?","Define a health metric for a ride-hailing driver marketplace."],"mistakes":["Choosing only engagement metrics and ignoring revenue and quality","Ignoring cannibalisation and guardrails","Forgetting how to evaluate without an experiment"],"practice":"Answer each of the four prompts aloud using the six-step framework, then compare with the model answers.","related":["m13-metrics","m6-lifecycle","m6-advanced"],"points":"1. Goal of the feature and users targeted.\n2. Primary metric (north star link), secondary, guardrails.\n3. Leading vs lagging indicators.\n4. Experiment design (A/B or quasi-experiment).\n5. Segments to inspect; potential cannibalization.\n6. Decision criteria and next steps.\n\nQ: How would you measure the success of \"Swiggy One\" / UPI cashback / a new \"reorder\" button?\nA: \n\nQ: Should Zomato increase delivery fees in rainy weather? What data do you need?\nA: \n\nQ: How do you evaluate a loyalty program's impact if you cannot run an A/B test?\nA: \n\nQ: Define a health metric for a ride-hailing driver marketplace.\nA: "},"intuitive":"<p>&quot;How would you measure whether this feature is a success?&quot; has a recipe. First say what the feature is for and who it is for. Then choose one main number that shows the feature is working, a few supporting numbers that explain why, and some &quot;do no harm&quot; numbers. Decide how you will test it (usually an A/B test) and what you will compare. Think about what it might steal from elsewhere (cannibalisation), and decide beforehand what result means &quot;keep it&quot;.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Product and analytics interviews use this prompt constantly, with different features.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Goal, primary / secondary / guardrail metrics, leading vs lagging, experiment design, segments and cannibalisation, decision criteria.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;To measure the success of a feature, I start with its purpose: what user problem and business goal does it serve? Then metrics. A primary success metric tied to that goal, adoption metrics like the share of users who try it and repeat, input metrics showing it is used well, and guardrails for side effects, such as cannibalisation or slower performance. Then the method: an A/B test if I can randomise; otherwise a staged rollout compared against holdouts, or a before-and-after with caution. I define in advance what result would make us ship, iterate or kill it. The catch: usage of the feature alone is not success; it must move the goal metric without harming others.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Why \"users of the feature buy more\" proves little","html":"<p>A &quot;reorder in one tap&quot; button launched to everyone. Data: customers who used it place 6 orders a month; those who did not place 3.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The tempting conclusion:</strong> &quot;The button doubled orders.&quot; Wrong.</li>\n<li><strong>Ask who chose to use it.</strong> Frequent customers are the ones most likely to reorder, so they were always heavier buyers. That is <strong>selection bias</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Look at the same two groups before launch:</strong> future users ordered 5.8 a month; future non-users ordered 3.0.</li>\n<li><strong>Difference in differences:</strong> users went from 5.8 to 6.0 (+0.2); non-users from 3.0 to 3.0 (0). The effect is about <strong>+0.2 orders a month</strong> per user, not +3.</li>\n<li><strong>Is +0.2 worth it?</strong> Multiply by the number of users and the margin per order, and compare with the cost of maintaining the feature.</li>\n<li><strong>Next time, plan a holdout:</strong> launch to 95% of users and keep 5% without the button for a month. That gives a clean causal estimate.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Adoption shows who likes a feature, not what it changed. Compare against a baseline: an experiment, a holdout, or at least the same people before the launch.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-framework","text":"The framework","level":3},{"id":"h-model-answers-to-the-practice-prompts","text":"Model answers to the practice prompts","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ol>\n<li>Goal of the feature and users targeted.</li>\n<li><strong>Primary metric</strong> (north star link), <strong>secondary</strong>, <strong>guardrails</strong>.</li>\n<li>Leading vs lagging indicators.</li>\n<li>Experiment design (A/B or quasi-experiment).</li>\n<li>Segments to inspect; potential cannibalization.</li>\n<li>Decision criteria and next steps.</li>\n</ol>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"13.3#1","src":"c","q":"How would you measure the success of &quot;Swiggy One&quot; / UPI cashback / a new &quot;reorder&quot; button?","a":"<p><strong>Goal and users:</strong> make repeat ordering effortless for returning customers who order similar items. <strong>Primary metric:</strong> reorders per active customer per week (or share of orders that are reorders); link to north star: orders per customer per month. <strong>Secondary:</strong> reorder-button click-through, time from app open to order, AOV, conversion of sessions using the button. <strong>Guardrails:</strong> cancellation and complaint rate (wrong or unavailable items reordered), delivery time, new-user conversion (is the button distracting?), long-run search/discovery engagement (cannibalisation).</p>\n<p><strong>Design:</strong> randomise eligible returning users (user-level) 50/50 for 2+ weeks; analyse orders per user (not sessions). <strong>Leading vs lagging:</strong> 7-day reorder rate (leading) and 30/90-day retention and order frequency (lagging). <strong>Segments:</strong> frequency tiers, cities, categories (groceries vs restaurants). <strong>Decision:</strong> ship if orders per customer rise by a meaningful margin (for example +2%), guardrails hold, and the gain does not come at the cost of new-customer discovery.</p>\n","s":"State the goal; primary metric (such as orders per user or retention), adoption, guardrails (margin, cannibalisation); test with an A/B experiment against a holdout."},{"k":"qa","id":"13.3#2","src":"c","q":"Should Zomato increase delivery fees in rainy weather? What data do you need?","a":"<p><strong>Clarify the goal:</strong> manage supply-demand balance in rain (rider availability drops and delivery times rise), protect service quality and profitability, without losing too many orders. <strong>Data needed:</strong> rider supply and demand by zone and hour, historical rain impact on delivery time and cancellations, price elasticity of demand (from past fee changes or experiments), customer segments and sensitivity, competitor pricing, and the cost of delays (refunds, churn).</p>\n<p><strong>Approach:</strong> test with a <strong>geo or switchback experiment</strong> (randomising individual customers is risky because demand and supply are shared between groups): raise the fee in matched zones on rainy hours, compare orders, delivery time, cancellations, rider earnings and retention against control zones. <strong>Metrics:</strong> contribution margin per order, completed orders, delivery time P90, customer complaints and repeat rate over following weeks. <strong>Risks:</strong> perceived unfairness and churn, regulatory and PR concerns, fee changes that only shift demand. <strong>Decision:</strong> adopt a dynamic fee if margin and service quality improve without harmful repeat-rate decline; consider alternatives such as rider incentives or capped surge.</p>\n","s":"Estimate demand elasticity and supply response: experiment by city or time, track orders, rider availability, delivery time, cancellations, and long-term retention, not just short-term revenue.","m":"<p><strong>Data needed.</strong> Weather and demand by area and hour, rider supply and acceptance rates, delivery times, price sensitivity from past fee changes, and competitor pricing. Compare the extra fee revenue to lost orders and long-term customer churn.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"13.3#3","src":"c","q":"How do you evaluate a loyalty program&#39;s impact if you cannot run an A/B test?","a":"<p>Without an A/B test, build a credible counterfactual. Options: (1) <strong>Staggered rollout</strong> as a natural experiment (compare cities or cohorts joining at different times, difference-in-differences). (2) <strong>Matching or propensity scores</strong> to compare members with similar non-members on pre-programme behaviour (recency, frequency, spend, city, tenure), remembering <strong>self-selection</strong>: people who join loyalty programmes were already more engaged, so naive comparisons overstate impact. (3) <strong>Regression discontinuity</strong> if eligibility has a threshold (for example a spend cutoff). (4) <strong>Synthetic control</strong> at a market level.</p>\n<p><strong>Metrics:</strong> incremental orders and margin per customer (net of benefits cost), retention, and cannibalisation of full-price purchases. Validate with <strong>pre-period parallel trends</strong> and placebo tests, report uncertainty, and be explicit that observational estimates are weaker than randomised ones. If possible, run a <strong>small holdout</strong> (a randomly withheld group) going forward to get a clean measurement.</p>\n","s":"Use quasi-experiments: matched members versus non-members, difference-in-differences or staggered rollout; adjust for self-selection and state the assumptions.","m":"<p><strong>Why selection matters.</strong> People who join a loyalty program are already heavier users, so a naive comparison overstates its effect. Matching on pre-program behaviour or difference-in-differences controls for that.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"13.3#4","src":"c","q":"Define a health metric for a ride-hailing driver marketplace.","a":"<p>A marketplace&#39;s health depends on both sides, so define a <strong>health metric for drivers</strong> linked to supply quality: (1) <strong>Earnings per online hour</strong> (and its distribution: median and lower percentile, not only the average), (2) <strong>utilisation</strong> (share of online time spent on trips), (3) <strong>retention and churn</strong> of active drivers (weekly active drivers, cohort retention), (4) <strong>acceptance and cancellation rates</strong>, (5) <strong>driver satisfaction</strong> (NPS, complaints, safety incidents).</p>\n<p><strong>Composite view:</strong> track earnings per hour, utilisation and 4-week retention together; a rise in average earnings with falling retention signals a problem. <strong>Balance with rider side:</strong> wait times, ETAs and price; over-incentivising drivers raises costs, too few drivers raises wait times. <strong>Guardrails:</strong> fairness across segments and cities, safety and compliance. Monitor leading indicators (acceptance, time between trips) for early warning, and run experiments (incentive changes) with marketplace-aware designs.</p>\n","s":"A basket of drivers&#39; health: active drivers, utilisation (paid time share), earnings per hour, retention and cancellation rate, with balance against rider wait times."},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-framework\">The framework</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Goal of the feature and the users targeted.</strong> What behaviour should change, for whom?</li>\n<li><strong>Primary metric</strong> (linked to the company&#39;s north star), <strong>secondary</strong> (diagnostic) and <strong>guardrail</strong> (must not degrade) metrics.</li>\n<li><strong>Leading vs lagging indicators:</strong> quick signals (feature usage, repeat within 7 days) that predict slower outcomes (retention at 90 days, LTV).</li>\n<li><strong>Experiment design:</strong> randomised A/B test; or a quasi-experiment if randomisation is impossible.</li>\n<li><strong>Segments and cannibalisation:</strong> who benefits (new vs returning, city tier), and what it might replace (a reorder button may cannibalise search but increase order frequency).</li>\n<li><strong>Decision criteria and next steps:</strong> ship, iterate, or kill, with thresholds set in advance.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"h-model-answers-to-the-practice-prompts\">Model answers to the practice prompts</h3>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<aside class=\"callout example\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Worked example</div><div class=\"callout-b\"><p><strong>Worked case: a &quot;save for later&quot; button on a shopping app.</strong> Goal: increase eventual purchases. Primary metric: purchases per user within 14 days. Adoption: percentage of product-page viewers who save, and who save again within a week. Quality: share of saved items later bought. Guardrails: immediate add-to-cart rate (are saves replacing carts?), page speed, revenue per user. Method: A/B test by user for two or more weeks. Decision rule agreed beforehand: ship if purchases per user rises by at least 1% with guardrails flat; iterate if saves are high but conversion is flat (users save but never return, so add reminders); kill if cart additions fall without a purchase gain.</p>\n</div></aside>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m13-feature-success~ca0_1","q":"Feature adoption is 40%, but revenue per user is flat. What might it mean?","a":"<p>Users are using it but it is not creating new value: it may substitute an existing behaviour (cannibalisation) or be a novelty. Check against a control group.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m13-feature-success~ca0_2","q":"What is a guardrail for a new recommendation feature?","a":"<p>Page load time, bounce rate, and revenue per session, so that engagement gains do not hide other harm.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m13-feature-success~ca0_3","q":"You cannot A/B test a feature (shipped to all). How do you estimate impact?","a":"<p>Use a staggered rollout or holdout if possible; otherwise difference-in-differences or an interrupted time series, stating the assumptions and limits.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m13-feature-success~ta2_1","q":"<p>A loyalty programme&#39;s members spend 4,000 a month and non-members 1,500. Before joining, the members spent 3,500 and the non-members 1,400 over the same months. Estimate the programme&#39;s effect and name the assumption you rely on.</p>\n","a":"<p>Members changed by 4,000 − 3,500 = +500; non-members by 1,500 − 1,400 = +100. Difference in differences: 500 − 100 = +400 a month per member, far less than the raw gap of 2,500. The assumption is parallel trends: without the programme, members&#39; spending would have grown by the same 100 as non-members&#39;. Check it with several months of pre-programme data.</p>\n"}]},"m13-problem-framing":{"id":"m13-problem-framing","slug":"ml-problem-framing-from-business-ask-to-ds-problem","m":13,"title":"ML problem framing: from business ask to DS problem","min":30,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":["m8-metric-choice"],"tags":["case-study","machine-learning","business"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Turning a business request into a machine learning problem: the four framing questions, a worked churn example, and the framing table for common business asks.","keyTerms":["problem framing","target definition","prediction time","features available at prediction time","cost of errors","action","success metric"],"interviewQs":["\"Reduce churn\": how do you turn it into an ML problem?","What questions do you ask before modelling?","How do you connect model metrics to business impact?"],"mistakes":["Starting with the algorithm","Vague target definitions (what is churn exactly?)","Optimising AUC instead of the action's value"],"practice":"Frame \"reduce delivery delays\" as an ML problem: target, prediction time, features, metric, action.","related":["m12-lifecycle","m8-metric-choice","m7-ensembling"],"points":"Business ask - Framing - Target - Key features - Metric - Action\n\"Reduce churn\" - Binary classification (churn in next 30 days) - churn flag - recency, frequency, support tickets, delivery delays - PR-AUC, lift at top 10%, expected profit - Targeted retention offers\n\"Reduce fraud\" - Classification/anomaly detection - fraud flag - velocity, device, geo, amount patterns - Recall at fixed FPR, PR-AUC - Block/step-up auth\n\"Forecast demand\" - Time-series regression - units per SKU-store-day - lags, promo, festival, price - WAPE, bias - Inventory planning\n\"Better ETAs\" - Regression - delivery time - distance, traffic, restaurant prep time, weather - MAE, P90 error, % late - Customer promise\n\"Segment customers\" - Clustering - none - RFM, categories, behavior - Silhouette + business profiling - Marketing strategy\n\"Approve loans\" - Credit scoring - default flag - bureau, income, behavior - KS, AUC, approval-vs-loss tradeoff - Approve/decline/limit\n\"Recommend items\" - Ranking - click/purchase - user/item embeddings, context - NDCG, CTR in A/B - Personalized feed\n\nAlways ask: What decision will this model change? What is the cost of each kind of error? What data is available at prediction time? How will success be measured?"},"intuitive":"<p>A business person says &quot;reduce churn&quot; or &quot;cut fraud&quot;. That is a wish, not a machine learning problem. Framing means turning it into a precise question a model can answer and a decision someone can act on: &quot;Among customers active today, who will stop ordering in the next 30 days, and which of them should we send an offer to?&quot; The four questions that do the work: what decision will this change, what does each kind of mistake cost, what data will I really have when I must predict, and how will we know it worked?</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Interviewers use vague asks to see whether you start with the business, not the algorithm.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Decision, cost of errors, data at prediction time, success measure.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Framing turns a vague business ask into a data science problem. I ask: what decision will this support, and who acts on it? What is the target and how is it defined? What is the metric of success and the cost of each error type? What data exists at prediction time? Is it a prediction, a causal question, an optimisation or just a report? For example, &#39;reduce churn&#39; becomes: predict which active customers will cancel in the next thirty days, so that retention can target the top ten per cent with an offer, evaluated on retained revenue. Sometimes no ML is needed; a rule or a dashboard is better. The catch: a model with no action attached has no value.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Frame \"use AI to reduce delivery delays\"","html":"<ol>\n<li><strong>Find the decision.</strong> Delays can be reduced by (a) giving customers more honest time promises, (b) assigning riders better, or (c) helping kitchens prepare on time. Ask which lever the team controls. Suppose (b): the dispatch team chooses which rider takes each order.</li>\n<li><strong>Define the target precisely.</strong> For each candidate rider at assignment time, predict the minutes from assignment to delivery.</li>\n<li><strong>Define the prediction moment and the features available then:</strong> rider location and current load, restaurant preparation-time history, distance, time of day, weather. Nothing recorded after assignment.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose metrics on two levels.</strong> Model: mean absolute error in minutes. Business: the share of orders delivered later than promised, and average delivery time.</li>\n<li><strong>Name the baseline:</strong> the current rule, &quot;nearest free rider&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>Plan the test:</strong> a switchback design (alternate the new and old assignment logic by time block within each city), because riders are shared between control and treatment.</li>\n<li><strong>Check whether ML is even needed:</strong> if most delay comes from a few slow kitchens, a simple rule or a conversation with those restaurants may beat any model.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Framing turns a slogan into a decision, a target, a prediction moment, metrics, a baseline and a test. The model comes last.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-four-questions","text":"The four questions","level":3},{"id":"h-the-framing-table-with-reasoning","text":"The framing table, with reasoning","level":3},{"id":"h-a-worked-framing-reduce-churn","text":"A worked framing: &quot;reduce churn&quot;","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Business ask</th><th>Framing</th><th>Target</th><th>Key features</th><th>Metric</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;Reduce churn&quot;</td><td>Binary classification (churn in next 30 days)</td><td>churn flag</td><td>recency, frequency, support tickets, delivery delays</td><td>PR-AUC, lift at top 10%, expected profit</td><td>Targeted retention offers</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Reduce fraud&quot;</td><td>Classification/anomaly detection</td><td>fraud flag</td><td>velocity, device, geo, amount patterns</td><td>Recall at fixed FPR, PR-AUC</td><td>Block/step-up auth</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Forecast demand&quot;</td><td>Time-series regression</td><td>units per SKU-store-day</td><td>lags, promo, festival, price</td><td>WAPE, bias</td><td>Inventory planning</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Better ETAs&quot;</td><td>Regression</td><td>delivery time</td><td>distance, traffic, restaurant prep time, weather</td><td>MAE, P90 error, % late</td><td>Customer promise</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Segment customers&quot;</td><td>Clustering</td><td>none</td><td>RFM, categories, behavior</td><td>Silhouette + business profiling</td><td>Marketing strategy</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Approve loans&quot;</td><td>Credit scoring</td><td>default flag</td><td>bureau, income, behavior</td><td>KS, AUC, approval-vs-loss tradeoff</td><td>Approve/decline/limit</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Recommend items&quot;</td><td>Ranking</td><td>click/purchase</td><td>user/item embeddings, context</td><td>NDCG, CTR in A/B</td><td>Personalized feed</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p>Always ask: <em>What decision will this model change? What is the cost of each kind of error? What data is available at prediction time? How will success be measured?</em></p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-four-questions\">The four questions</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>What decision will this model change?</strong> (who gets an offer, which transaction is blocked, how much stock to order)</li>\n<li><strong>What is the cost of each kind of error?</strong> (a wasted discount vs a lost customer; blocking a good customer vs missing fraud)</li>\n<li><strong>What data is available at prediction time?</strong> (and how fresh). This defines the snapshot date and prevents leakage.</li>\n<li><strong>How will success be measured?</strong> A model metric (PR-AUC, lift) <em>and</em> a business measure (incremental retention in an A/B test, money saved).</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"h-the-framing-table-with-reasoning\">The framing table, with reasoning</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Business ask</th><th>Framing</th><th>Target</th><th>Key features</th><th>Metric</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;Reduce churn&quot;</td><td>binary classification: churn in next 30 days</td><td>churn flag</td><td>recency, frequency, support tickets, delivery delays</td><td>PR-AUC, lift at top 10%, expected profit</td><td>targeted retention offers</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Reduce fraud&quot;</td><td>classification / anomaly detection</td><td>fraud flag</td><td>velocity, device, geo, amount patterns</td><td>recall at fixed FPR, PR-AUC</td><td>block or step-up authentication</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Forecast demand&quot;</td><td>time-series regression</td><td>units per SKU-store-day</td><td>lags, promo, festival, price</td><td>WAPE, bias</td><td>inventory planning</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Better ETAs&quot;</td><td>regression</td><td>delivery time</td><td>distance, traffic, restaurant prep time, weather</td><td>MAE, P90 error, % late</td><td>customer promise</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Segment customers&quot;</td><td>clustering</td><td>none</td><td>RFM, categories, behaviour</td><td>silhouette + business profiling</td><td>marketing strategy</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Approve loans&quot;</td><td>credit scoring</td><td>default flag</td><td>bureau, income, behaviour</td><td>KS, AUC, approval-vs-loss trade-off</td><td>approve / decline / set limit</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Recommend items&quot;</td><td>ranking</td><td>click or purchase</td><td>user and item embeddings, context</td><td>NDCG, CTR in an A/B test</td><td>personalised feed</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-a-worked-framing-reduce-churn\">A worked framing: &quot;reduce churn&quot;</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Entity and snapshot:</strong> every active customer on the first of each month.</li>\n<li><strong>Target:</strong> no order in the following 30 days <em>(define &quot;active&quot; = at least one order in the previous 90 days)</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Features (as of the snapshot):</strong> recency, order frequency and trend, average order value, discount reliance, late-delivery and cancellation rates, support contacts, app engagement, city tier, tenure.</li>\n<li><strong>Validation:</strong> time-based split (train on earlier months, test on the latest); do not randomly split customers across time.</li>\n<li><strong>Model and metric:</strong> gradient boosting vs logistic baseline; PR-AUC and <strong>lift at the top decile</strong>, then <strong>expected profit</strong> = uplift × customer value − offer cost over targeted customers.</li>\n<li><strong>Action:</strong> the retention team can contact 10% of customers each week; send the offer to the top 10% by <em>expected incremental value</em>. Because a prediction of churn is not a prediction that an offer will <em>work</em>, plan a <strong>holdout group</strong> to measure the real effect, and consider an <strong>uplift model</strong> later.</li>\n<li><strong>Deployment and monitoring:</strong> weekly batch scoring; track drift, offer cost and retained revenue; retrain monthly.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m13-problem-framing~1","q":"The head of operations says &quot;predict late deliveries&quot;. How do you frame it and what do you ask?","a":"<p>Ask: <strong>when</strong> must the prediction be made (at order placement, at pickup?) and therefore which features exist; <strong>what action</strong> follows (extend the promised time, assign a different rider, alert the customer, compensate); what &quot;late&quot; means (promised vs actual, tolerance); and the <strong>costs</strong> (a padded ETA lowers conversion; missed lateness raises complaints). Frame as <strong>regression</strong> on delivery time (with prediction intervals or the P90 to set promises) or <strong>classification</strong> of late deliveries. Features: distance, restaurant prep-time history, rider availability, traffic, weather, order size, time of day. Metrics: MAE, P90 error, % of orders late, and the business effect in an experiment.</p>\n","s":"Clarify when the prediction is needed, what action follows, and the cost of false alarms vs misses; frame as regression on delivery time or classification of &quot;late&quot;."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m13-problem-framing~ca0_1","q":"&quot;Increase sales with AI.&quot; What clarifying questions do you ask?","a":"<p>Which decision, which sales (segment, channel), what lever can change (price, recommendations, outreach), what data exists, how success is measured, and the constraints.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m13-problem-framing~ca0_2","q":"Predict churn or estimate the effect of a discount: what is the difference?","a":"<p>Prediction ranks who will churn; the discount question is causal (uplift): who changes behaviour because of the offer. Targeting the likely churners can waste money on those who would stay anyway.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m13-problem-framing~ca0_3","q":"When would you recommend not using ML?","a":"<p>When a simple rule or report meets the need, there is little data or signal, or errors are too costly to risk without explanation.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m13-problem-framing~ta1_1","q":"<p>A manager asks you to &quot;predict which new restaurants will become popular&quot;. Write the framing in four lines: decision, target, prediction moment, metric.</p>\n","a":"<p>Decision: which new restaurants get promotional placement in their first month. Target: whether a restaurant reaches the top 30% of its city by orders in months 2 and 3. Prediction moment: day 14 after onboarding, using menu, prices, cuisine, location, ratings and orders from the first two weeks only. Metric: precision among the top 20 restaurants chosen each month, plus the order lift for promoted restaurants measured against a randomly held-out group.</p>\n"}]},"m13-guesstimates":{"id":"m13-guesstimates","slug":"guesstimates","m":13,"title":"Guesstimates","min":20,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":[],"tags":["case-study","estimation"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"How to structure a guesstimate (market sizing) question, with three worked examples, rules for choosing assumptions and sanity checks.","keyTerms":["guesstimate","market sizing","top-down","bottom-up","assumptions","sanity check"],"interviewQs":["How many food deliveries happen per day in Bengaluru?","How many UPI transactions happen in India per day?","How many tea stalls are there in Pune?"],"mistakes":["Silent arithmetic without stating assumptions","Unrealistic round numbers with no justification","Skipping the sanity check"],"practice":"Do three guesstimates under five minutes each and compare with a real reference you look up afterwards.","related":["m13-communication","m13-metrics"],"points":"Structure: clarify scope -> segment the population -> apply assumptions with rough numbers -> compute -> sanity-check.\n\nExample: \"How many food deliveries happen per day in Bengaluru?\"\nPopulation ~13M x share ordering regularly (~15%) ~ 2M people x ~2 orders/week / 7 = ~570k orders/day across all platforms. Sanity check vs known platform volumes; state assumptions explicitly."},"intuitive":"<p>A guesstimate is a structured way to make a good guess when you do not have the data, like estimating how many balloons fit in a room by measuring one balloon and the room. You break the big unknown into smaller pieces you can reason about, put a sensible number on each, multiply, and then check whether the answer feels right. Interviewers are not testing whether you know the exact number; they are testing how you think and whether you state assumptions out loud.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Common at consulting-style analytics firms.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Clarify, segment the population, assume with numbers, compute, sanity-check.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;A guesstimate is a structured estimate from reasonable assumptions. I restate the question, choose an approach, split into parts I can estimate, state my assumptions with numbers, calculate, then sanity-check against something known. For example, how many coffee cups are sold in a city per day? Population one million, say forty per cent adults who drink coffee, two cups a day on average, but only half buy outside, so about 400,000 cups a day. I round numbers to make arithmetic easy and keep units. The catch: the interviewer is grading the structure and the sanity check, not the exact answer, so I talk through each assumption and say which I am least sure of.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Estimate a neighbourhood gym's monthly revenue, two ways","html":"<ol>\n<li><strong>Clarify:</strong> a mid-range gym in a large Indian city; revenue from memberships only.</li>\n<li><strong>Supply side (capacity).</strong> About 80 machines and stations. At the peak hour, 7 to 8 pm, maybe 80% are in use, so 64 people. Members come about 3 times a week and spread over roughly 6 busy hours, with the peak taking about a quarter of a day&#39;s visits. Visits a day ≈ 64 / 0.25 ≈ 250.</li>\n<li><strong>Visits to members.</strong> 250 visits a day × 7 days = 1,750 visits a week. At 3 visits per member: about <strong>580 members</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Revenue:</strong> 580 × 2,000 rupees a month (an average after discounts on annual plans) ≈ <strong>11.6 lakh a month</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Sanity check from the demand side.</strong> The catchment has perhaps 40,000 adults, of whom about 3% use some gym, and this gym has half the local market: 40,000 × 3% × 0.5 = 600 members. Close to 580.</li>\n<li><strong>State the weakest assumption:</strong> the peak-hour share. If the peak holds a third of daily visits, not a quarter, visits fall to about 190 a day, members to about 450 and revenue to about 9 lakh.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Break the quantity into parts you can estimate, keep numbers round, check with an independent method, and say which assumption moves the answer most.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-structure","text":"The structure","level":3},{"id":"h-worked-example-1-food-deliveries-per-day-in-beng","text":"Worked example 1: food deliveries per day in Bengaluru (the course example)","level":3},{"id":"h-worked-example-2-upi-transactions-per-day-in-ind","text":"Worked example 2: UPI transactions per day in India (top-down, from the user base)","level":3},{"id":"h-worked-example-3-tea-stalls-in-pune-bottom-up-by","text":"Worked example 3: tea stalls in Pune (bottom-up by area)","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p>Structure: clarify scope -&gt; segment the population -&gt; apply assumptions with rough numbers -&gt; compute -&gt; sanity-check.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p>Example: &quot;How many food deliveries happen per day in Bengaluru?&quot;\nPopulation <del>13M x share ordering regularly (</del>15%) ~ 2M people x ~2 orders/week / 7 = ~570k orders/day across all platforms. Sanity check vs known platform volumes; state assumptions explicitly.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-structure\">The structure</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Clarify scope:</strong> which city, which time frame, what counts (only app deliveries? meals or groceries?).</li>\n<li><strong>Segment the population</strong> or choose an approach: <strong>top-down</strong> (population × share) or <strong>bottom-up</strong> (supply side: restaurants × orders each).</li>\n<li><strong>Assume</strong> with rough, justified numbers; say them aloud.</li>\n<li><strong>Compute</strong> with simple arithmetic (round to make maths easy).</li>\n<li><strong>Sanity-check</strong> against a second approach or a known reference; state the main uncertainty.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"h-worked-example-1-food-deliveries-per-day-in-beng\">Worked example 1: food deliveries per day in Bengaluru (the course example)</h3>\n<p>Population of Bengaluru ≈ 13 million. Share who order food online regularly ≈ 15% → ~2 million people. Orders per person per week ≈ 2 → 4 million orders a week → ~570,000 orders a day across all platforms.</p>\n<p><em>Sanity check (bottom-up):</em> ~15,000 active delivery restaurants × ~35 orders a day ≈ 525,000. Two independent approaches land within 10%, so a range of <strong>0.5 to 0.6 million</strong> a day is reasonable.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-worked-example-2-upi-transactions-per-day-in-ind\">Worked example 2: UPI transactions per day in India (top-down, from the user base)</h3>\n<p>Active UPI users ≈ 350 million. Suppose an average active user makes about 1 transaction a day (many make 3 to 5, many less than once). → ~350 million a day.</p>\n<p><em>Sanity check:</em> public monthly figures have been in the many billions of transactions, which is on the order of hundreds of millions a day, consistent with the estimate. State the key uncertainty (the average frequency per user) and the sensitivity: if the average is 1.5, the estimate is ~525 million.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-worked-example-3-tea-stalls-in-pune-bottom-up-by\">Worked example 3: tea stalls in Pune (bottom-up by area)</h3>\n<p>Pune population ≈ 7 million. Suppose one stall serves about 400 to 500 people in a typical catchment area (commuters, students, offices) → 7,000,000 / 450 ≈ <strong>15,000 stalls</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Sanity check:</em> density of ~1 stall per 450 residents is plausible for a dense Indian city; cross-check by sampling one neighbourhood in your head (a street with 6 stalls in 1 km²; Pune&#39;s urban area of about 500 km² with lower density in suburbs gives a similar ballpark).</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<aside class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Make the arithmetic easy</div><div class=\"callout-b\"><p>Use round numbers (13 million, 15%), keep the ratios clean, and say your assumption before you use it. If an interviewer challenges one, swap the number and show how the answer changes (sensitivity): it demonstrates structure more than the final figure does.</p>\n</div></aside>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m13-guesstimates~1","q":"Estimate the number of smartphones sold in India in a year.","a":"<p>A structured try: India ≈ 1.4 billion people, about 55% (≈ 770 million) own a smartphone, replacement every ~4 years → ~190 million a year. <em>Sanity check:</em> published market shipments are often quoted around 150 to 160 million a year; the estimate is in the right ballpark. The key uncertainty is the <strong>replacement cycle</strong> (3 to 5 years) and ownership share; say so, and show the range (155 to 260 million). Good guesstimates report a <strong>range</strong> and the main driver of uncertainty.</p>\n","s":"About 190 million: 1.4 billion people, roughly 55% owning a smartphone, replaced about every 4 years."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m13-guesstimates~ca0_1","q":"Estimate the number of petrol stations in a city of 2 million.","a":"<p>About 1 car per 4 people gives 500,000 cars; each fills up about once a week = 70,000 fills/day; one station serves roughly 500/day, so about 140 stations. Sanity-check with local knowledge.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m13-guesstimates~ca0_2","q":"Why state assumptions explicitly?","a":"<p>It shows structure, lets the interviewer correct a number, and makes the answer traceable and adjustable.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m13-guesstimates~ca0_3","q":"What is a sanity check for a market-size estimate?","a":"<p>Compare with a top-down or known benchmark (revenue of a known company, per-capita spend) to see if the order of magnitude is plausible.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m13-guesstimates~ta1_1","q":"<p>Estimate the number of orders a single cloud kitchen in a busy area handles per day. Give your structure and a sanity check.</p>\n","a":"<p>One structure, by capacity: 2 cooking lines × 4 orders in parallel per line × an average cooking time of 12 minutes gives 2 × 4 × 5 = 40 orders an hour at full speed. Busy periods total about 5 hours a day (lunch and dinner) at about 70% utilisation: 40 × 5 × 0.7 ≈ 140 orders, plus perhaps 30 in quieter hours, so about 170 a day. Sanity check by revenue: at an average of 350 rupees, that is about 60,000 rupees a day, or 18 lakh a month, which is plausible for a busy kitchen. The weakest assumption is the number of parallel orders per line.</p>\n"}]},"m13-communication":{"id":"m13-communication","slug":"communication-rules-for-data-scientists","m":13,"title":"Communication rules for data scientists","min":10,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":[],"tags":["communication","soft-skills"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Communication rules for data scientists: lead with the answer, translate to business impact, state assumptions and limits, and tailor depth to the audience, with before-and-after examples.","keyTerms":["BLUF","bottom line up front","tailoring to audience","one chart per message","assumptions","limitations"],"interviewQs":["How do you explain a model's result to a non-technical executive?","How do you present bad news or an inconclusive result?"],"mistakes":["Starting with methodology","Reporting statistical output without business meaning","Overloading slides with charts"],"practice":"Rewrite one of your project summaries as a three-sentence message for an executive.","related":["m13-metric-drop","m15-tell-me"],"points":"- Lead with the answer (BLUF), then evidence.\n- Translate metrics to business impact (Rs, users, hours).\n- State assumptions, limitations, and confidence.\n- Use one chart per message; annotate the takeaway.\n- Tailor depth to audience: executives (decision + impact), product managers (trade-offs), engineers (details)."},"intuitive":"<p>Good analysis that nobody understands changes nothing. The rule is to put the answer first, then the evidence: &quot;We should fix the Android payment bug; it cost us 40,000 orders this week&quot;, then a chart that shows it, then the details for anyone who wants them. Talk in rupees, users and hours, not p-values and AUCs, unless your listener is another data scientist. And always say how sure you are and what you did not check.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Communication is half of the job and a major factor in hiring decisions.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Answer first, impact in business terms, one chart per message, assumptions and limits stated.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Communication is a core data science skill. I lead with the answer or recommendation, then the supporting evidence, then the caveats. I tailor to the audience: executives want the decision and impact; engineers want the details. I use one idea per slide, a clear chart with a takeaway title, and plain language, defining terms. I quantify uncertainty honestly with ranges rather than false precision. When asked something I do not know, I say so and explain how I would find out. In an interview I structure answers, such as situation, approach, result, and check in with the listener. The catch: the model&#39;s accuracy is not the message; what changes because of it is.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Rewrite an analysis update for an executive","html":"<p><strong>Before:</strong>\n<em>&quot;We pulled six months of data from the events table, cleaned the duplicates, built cohorts by signup week and ran a logistic regression with 14 features. AUC was 0.78. Interestingly, the onboarding tutorial completion variable had the largest coefficient. We also tried XGBoost.&quot;</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Find the answer the reader needs:</strong> what should we do, and why?</li>\n<li><strong>Lead with it:</strong> &quot;Users who finish the onboarding tutorial are about twice as likely to still be active after 30 days. I recommend making the tutorial shorter and testing it as the default.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Give the two or three strongest supporting facts, in business terms:</strong> &quot;Only 35% finish it today. Among users who joined in the same week and city, finishers retain at 48% against 24%.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>State the caution honestly:</strong> &quot;This is an association: motivated users may simply be more likely to finish. The A/B test will tell us the true effect.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>End with the ask and the next step:</strong> &quot;Approve a two-week test from Monday; we will report the effect on 30-day retention by the 20th.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Move the method to an appendix:</strong> the data, the model and the AUC are there for anyone who wants them.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Answer first, then evidence, then caveat, then the ask. The analysis is not finished until someone knows what to decide.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-rules-with-before-and-after","text":"The rules, with before and after","level":3},{"id":"h-a-template-for-a-short-update","text":"A template for a short update","level":3},{"id":"h-delivering-bad-or-inconclusive-news","text":"Delivering bad or inconclusive news","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Lead with the answer</strong> (BLUF), then evidence.</li>\n<li>Translate metrics to business impact (Rs, users, hours).</li>\n<li>State assumptions, limitations, and confidence.</li>\n<li>Use one chart per message; annotate the takeaway.</li>\n<li>Tailor depth to audience: executives (decision + impact), product managers (trade-offs), engineers (details).</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-rules-with-before-and-after\">The rules, with before and after</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Weak</th><th>Strong</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Lead with the answer (BLUF)</strong></td><td>&quot;We trained a gradient-boosting model on 12 months of data with 40 features...&quot;</td><td>&quot;Targeting the top 10% of customers by churn risk can retain about ₹1.2 crore a year. Here is how we know.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Translate to impact</strong></td><td>&quot;AUC improved from 0.78 to 0.83.&quot;</td><td>&quot;The new model finds 25% more at-risk customers for the same number of offers, worth about ₹40 lakh more per quarter.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td><strong>State assumptions, limits, confidence</strong></td><td>&quot;The feature increases conversion.&quot;</td><td>&quot;Conversion rose 1.2 points (95% CI 0.4 to 2.0) over two weeks; effects beyond a month and on new users are untested.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td><strong>One chart per message</strong></td><td>a dashboard of 9 charts</td><td>one chart titled &quot;Payment failures on Android 4.2 doubled after release&quot;</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tailor the depth</strong></td><td>the same deck for everyone</td><td>executives: decision and impact; product managers: trade-offs and options; engineers: details, edge cases</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-a-template-for-a-short-update\">A template for a short update</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Conclusion / recommendation</strong> (one sentence).</li>\n<li><strong>Why:</strong> two or three key facts with numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Confidence and caveats:</strong> what could change the conclusion.</li>\n<li><strong>Next step and ask:</strong> what you need from them and by when.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"h-delivering-bad-or-inconclusive-news\">Delivering bad or inconclusive news</h3>\n<p>State it plainly (&quot;the test did not show a detectable improvement&quot;), quantify what you can rule out (&quot;we can rule out gains above +0.6 points&quot;), explain what to do next (longer test, bigger change, different metric), and avoid blaming or over-apologising. An honest &quot;we don&#39;t know yet, and here is how we will find out&quot; builds more trust than a forced conclusion.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m13-communication~1","q":"Explain a random forest model&#39;s churn predictions to the marketing head in three sentences.","a":"<p>&quot;The model reviews each customer&#39;s recent ordering, delivery experience and support history and gives a churn risk score. The top 10% by score contain about three times more future churners than average, so focusing offers there is three times as efficient as contacting customers at random. The biggest warning signs are fewer orders over the last 30 days, repeated late deliveries and unresolved support tickets, so fixing those experiences also matters.&quot; This leads with the use, gives impact in plain terms and surfaces drivers without jargon.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m13-communication~ca0_1","q":"How do you present a model result to a non-technical executive?","a":"<p>Start with the decision and business impact, one clear chart, the main risk, and the recommended next step; keep metrics in business terms such as dollars saved.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m13-communication~ca0_2","q":"Stakeholders challenge your result. How do you respond?","a":"<p>Listen, restate their concern, show the evidence and assumptions, acknowledge limits, and offer a check that could settle the point.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m13-communication~ca0_3","q":"Why lead with the conclusion?","a":"<p>It respects the listener&#39;s time and attention, and everything after is read as support for a known point.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m13-communication~ta1_1","q":"<p>Rewrite for a sales director: &quot;The model&#39;s precision at the 0.6 threshold is 0.42 with recall 0.71, and the PR-AUC improved by 0.08 over baseline.&quot;</p>\n","a":"<p>&quot;Of the accounts the model flags as likely to leave, about 4 in 10 really are at risk, and it finds about 7 in 10 of all at-risk accounts. That is clearly better than our current rule. If the team calls the flagged accounts each week, we expect to reach most at-risk customers while about 6 in 10 calls go to customers who were fine.&quot; A number they can act on, in their terms, with the trade-off stated.</p>\n"}]}});